There  are  two  key  laws  in  Thailand  that  governs  electronic  commerce  activities.  The  Electronic Transactions Act B.E. 2544 (2001) (Thailand) is the enabling act for electronic transactions. The act includes broader elements related to electronic transactions such as the recognition of electronic signatures, the establishment of a governing body, and penalties of non-compliance to regulations that fall under the act (Laws of the Kingdom of Thailand, 2001).
The responsibilities of market participants involved in electronic transactions is detailed out in the regulations issued through royal decree namely the Royal Decree on the Operation of Digital Platform Service Business that are Subject to Prior Notification B.E. 2565 (2022).
Through the royal decree, digital platforms are required to register with the Electronic Transactions Development Agency. Digital platforms are required to have a local point of contact in the event where the operations are located outside of the country (Thailand Digital Platform Royal Decree, 2022). The obligations of digital platforms in creating a fair and transparent business environment includes the following
1. Publishing user services terms and conditions prior to the use of services. Publication to include details on
- a. terms  and  conditions  of  services,  service  suspension  or  termination,  and  charging  of  the remuneration;
- b. main parameters of algorithms or of criteria used by the operator to rank or recommend to users the list of goods or services;
- c. main parameters of algorithms or of criteria used by the operator to present an advertisement of goods or services to users;
- d. main parameters of algorithms or of criteria used by the operator to collect, moderate and publish user reviews;
- e. access to and use of data received from the provision of the digital platform service by the operator or user;
- f. support channels, internal complaint-handling system, and dispute resolution, as well as the timeframe of such action;
- g. rating of goods, services, or contents that are appropriate to each group of users; (8) actions to be taken to illegal goods, services, or contents
2. Platforms with revenues exceeding THB300 million and user base exceeding 10% of the Thai population are required to comply to the following
- a. Conduct risk assessments and duty to implement measures to mitigate risk
- b. Annual submission of compliance report to the regulating agency

